 Certificate of"},{"id":"FamilyLifeandRelations","title":"Family Life and Relations"},{"id":"Foreigner","title":"Foreigner"},{"id":"GleanGleaning","title":"Glean, Gleaning"},{"id":"HammurabiLawCodeof","title":"Hammurabi*, Law Code of"},{"id":"KingsBooksofFirstandSecond","title":"Kings, Books of First and Second"},{"id":"LawBiblicalConceptof","title":"Law, Biblical Concept of"},{"id":"MarriageMarriageCustoms","title":"Marriage, Marriage Customs"},{"id":"Mill","title":"Mill"},{"id":"Neighbor","title":"Neighbor"},{"id":"Orphan","title":"Orphan"},{"id":"Poorthe","title":"Poor, the"},{"id":"PriestsandLevites","title":"Priests and Levites"},{"id":"Ransom","title":"Ransom"},{"id":"SlaveSlavery","title":"Slave, Slavery"},{"id":"Teacher","title":"Teacher"},{"id":"Warfare","title":"Warfare"},{"id":"Widow","title":"Widow"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"then the husband who divorced her first may not remarry her after she has been defiled, for that is an abomination to the LORD. You must not bring sin upon the land that the LORD your God is giving you as an inheritance.","verse":4},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Remember what the LORD your God did to Miriam on the journey after you came out of Egypt.","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"be sure to return it to him by sunset, so that he may sleep in his own cloak and bless you, and this will be credited to you as righteousness before the LORD your God.","verse":13},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"You are to pay his wages each day before sunset, because he is poor and depends on them. Otherwise he may cry out to the LORD against you, and you will be guilty of sin.","verse":15},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Remember that you were slaves in Egypt, and the LORD your God redeemed you from that place. Therefore I am commanding you to do this.","verse":18},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"If you are harvesting in your field and forget a sheaf there, do not go back to get it. It is to be left for the foreigner, the fatherless, and the widow, so that the LORD your God may bless you in all the work of your hands.","verse":19}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Miriam","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Miriam::Miriam::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Miriam"},"text":"Remember what the LORD your God did to Miriam on the journey after you came out of Egypt.","verse":9}],"id":"Miriam","label":"Miriam","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Miriam::Miriam::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Miriam","term":"Miriam"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Miriam","sourceTitle":"Miriam"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.1","r":"Deut.24.1","rawR":"Deut.24.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.1","value":"Deut.24.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["something wrong:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This phrase indicates sexual impurity or some other impropriety. It does not pertain to adultery, which would have required the death penalty (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.22.22","r":"Deut.22.22","rawR":"Deut.22.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.22.22","value":"Deut.22.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"22:22"},"). • ",{"c":["a document of divorce:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This procedure was a concession; initiating a divorce in such cases was optional and not necessarily desirable (see ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.2.16","r":"Mal.2.16","rawR":"Mal.2.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.2.16","value":"Mal.2.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mal 2:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.5.31-32","r":"Matt.5.31-32","rawR":"Matt.5.31-32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.5.31-32","value":"Matt.5.31-32"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 5:31-32"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mark.10.2-12","r":"Mark.10.2-12","rawR":"Mark.10.2-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mark.10.2-12","value":"Mark.10.2-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mark 10:2-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.1","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"aa06d1b76569e507eb53078fdd570b6adaa012fca339edf1f8664152f25c0bd8","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2322,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.4","r":"Deut.24.4","rawR":"Deut.24.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.4","value":"Deut.24.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Perhaps the woman is described as ",{"c":["defiled"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," because she had two husbands; returning to the first husband would be considered adultery. • ",{"c":["guilt upon the land:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The concept of community was so strong in ancient Israel that the individual’s identity almost merged with that of the community. The sin of one individual had collective, corporate consequences."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.4","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b6d7662919fa040ba04c85efa9d0637dd2b419af1390788fe81095f31dec39a2","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2323,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.6","r":"Deut.24.6","rawR":"Deut.24.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.6","value":"Deut.24.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["the owner uses it to make a living"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["it is his life"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): Without his tool, the owner of the millstone might perish. The law imposed a limit on what could secure a loan. The millstone probably represents any means by which an individual made his living."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.6","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"86d0bdb60db720210954231c1f8bc1f4e6e23cd3596977f9b94a3696e602e016","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2324,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.8","r":"Deut.24.8","rawR":"Deut.24.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.8","value":"Deut.24.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["serious skin diseases:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Medical science has determined that this does not refer to leprosy (i.e., Hansen’s disease) per se."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.8","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1c45e35153c515492b1b02f449d1d1e387925f9f3cd0edcb13be59707478ec4e","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2325,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.11","r":"Deut.24.11","rawR":"Deut.24.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.11","value":"Deut.24.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["wait outside:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Even debtors were to be allowed their dignity and spared unnecessary embarrassment. A creditor was not to invade the debtor’s privacy when collecting from him."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.11","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"71422bc2068f612ae612e03869b27a19de6c8bfdbc85b528858e0c56afedb9e6","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2326,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.13","r":"Deut.24.13","rawR":"Deut.24.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.13","value":"Deut.24.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Return the cloak . . . by sunset:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The law was merciful."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.13","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"bb6dd94a8134be402605635e2afca2e9d815430e9a1e2bcf8651a5d4db337724","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2327,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.17","r":"Deut.24.17","rawR":"Deut.24.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.17","value":"Deut.24.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:17"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["True justice must be given"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["do not deprive of justice"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): The people of Israel were to vigorously resist discrimination against the weakest and most vulnerable people in society. Israel had been redeemed from such a status in Egypt, and their experience should help them realize how to treat others in similar circumstances (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.24.18","r":"Deut.24.18","rawR":"Deut.24.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.24.18","value":"Deut.24.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:18"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.24.17","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":24,"endVerse":17,"startChapter":24,"startVerse":17}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.24.17","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[24],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"18cccfff782ab890937ced44995b091e58ef9a378f3571c8a98ecccae5214f56","sourceItemName":"Deut.24.17","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2328,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.24.17","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.24.17","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}